tongxin-187:
**Key idea:** Use a packed `struct` (plus enums) when an instruction is just decoded fields: compact, synthesizable, value semantics. Use a class hierarchy when each instruction kind has its own behavior (execute, disassemble, constraints) that you want to extend, randomize, and override through the factory. In practice RTL uses structs and the testbench uses classes.

| Aspect | `struct packed` | class hierarchy |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Synthesizable | yes | no |
| Copy semantics | value (assignment copies) | handle (need `copy()`/`clone()`) |
| Per-opcode behavior | `case (op)` in one big function | `virtual execute()` per subclass |
| Randomization | `rand` struct inside a class | per-class constraints, `rand` fields |
| Extensibility | edit the case statement | add a subclass and a factory override |
| Memory/speed | very cheap | object allocation per instruction |

### Code
```systemverilog
// ---- RTL / decode view ----
typedef enum logic [6:0] {OP_R=7'b0110011, OP_I=7'b0010011, OP_LD=7'b0000011} opcode_e;
typedef struct packed {
  logic [6:0] funct7;
  logic [4:0] rs2, rs1;
  logic [2:0] funct3;
  logic [4:0] rd;
  opcode_e    opcode;
} rtype_t;                                  // 32 bits, overlay on instr word

// ---- verification view ----
virtual class instr extends uvm_object;
  rand bit [4:0] rd, rs1;
  function new(string name = "instr"); super.new(name); endfunction
  pure virtual function void execute(ref bit [31:0] rf[32]);
  pure virtual function string disasm();
endclass

class add_instr extends instr;
  `uvm_object_utils(add_instr)
  rand bit [4:0] rs2;
  function new(string name = "add_instr"); super.new(name); endfunction
  virtual function void execute(ref bit [31:0] rf[32]);
    if (rd != 0) rf[rd] = rf[rs1] + rf[rs2];
  endfunction
  virtual function string disasm();
    return $sformatf("add x%0d, x%0d, x%0d", rd, rs1, rs2);
  endfunction
endclass
```

### Explanation
- A reference model loops over `instr` handles and calls `execute()`. Adding a new opcode never touches that loop.
- A struct can still sit inside a class (`rand rtype_t f;`) for encoding and packing to bits.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Struct assignments copy, but class assignments share one object. Pushing the same handle into a queue twice is a classic bug.
- Unpacked structs cannot be cast to a bit vector. Use `packed` for encodings.

### Follow-ups
- *How do you randomize an instruction mix?* Generate the type via `randcase`/weights and build it with `instr::type_id::create`, or randomize an enum and use a factory lookup.

tongxin-188:
**Key idea:** A 2:1 mux is `y = sel ? b : a`. A 4:1 mux is either three 2:1 muxes in two levels (`sel[0]` picks within each pair, `sel[1]` picks between the pairs) or one fully specified `case`/array index. Parameterize the width and make every selector value drive a defined output.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  D0["d0"] --> M0["MUX2 (sel0)"]
  D1["d1"] --> M0
  D2["d2"] --> M1["MUX2 (sel0)"]
  D3["d3"] --> M1
  M0 --> M2["MUX2 (sel1)"]
  M1 --> M2
  M2 --> Y["y"]
```

| sel[1:0] | y |
| --- | --- |
| 00 | d0 |
| 01 | d1 |
| 10 | d2 |
| 11 | d3 |

### Code
```systemverilog
module mux2 #(parameter int W = 8) (
  input  logic [W-1:0] a, b,
  input  logic         sel,
  output logic [W-1:0] y
);
  assign y = sel ? b : a;
endmodule

// structural: three 2:1 muxes
module mux4_tree #(parameter int W = 8) (
  input  logic [W-1:0] d0, d1, d2, d3,
  input  logic [1:0]   sel,
  output logic [W-1:0] y
);
  logic [W-1:0] lo, hi;
  mux2 #(W) u0 (.a(d0), .b(d1), .sel(sel[0]), .y(lo));
  mux2 #(W) u1 (.a(d2), .b(d3), .sel(sel[0]), .y(hi));
  mux2 #(W) u2 (.a(lo), .b(hi), .sel(sel[1]), .y(y));
endmodule

// behavioral: one case statement
module mux4_case #(parameter int W = 8) (
  input  logic [W-1:0] d0, d1, d2, d3,
  input  logic [1:0]   sel,
  output logic [W-1:0] y
);
  always_comb begin
    unique case (sel)
      2'd0: y = d0;
      2'd1: y = d1;
      2'd2: y = d2;
      2'd3: y = d3;
    endcase
  end
endmodule

// self-checking test: every path, two data patterns
module tb;
  logic [7:0] d[4], y1, y2; logic [1:0] sel;
  mux4_tree u_t (.d0(d[0]), .d1(d[1]), .d2(d[2]), .d3(d[3]), .sel(sel), .y(y1));
  mux4_case u_c (.d0(d[0]), .d1(d[1]), .d2(d[2]), .d3(d[3]), .sel(sel), .y(y2));
  initial begin
    repeat (2) begin
      foreach (d[i]) d[i] = $urandom;
      for (int s = 0; s < 4; s++) begin
        sel = s; #1;
        if (y1 !== d[s] || y2 !== d[s]) $error("sel=%0d mismatch", s);
      end
    end
    $display("mux test done");
  end
endmodule
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- `sel = 2'bx`: `?:` merges the inputs bitwise (X where they differ), while `case` holds the old value in simulation. That is X-optimism. Consider an `assert (!$isunknown(sel))`.
- Missing a `default` in a non-full case infers a latch in `always @*`. `always_comb` plus `unique` flags it.
- Test with distinct, non-zero data on every input, or a wrong-path bug can hide.

### Follow-ups
- *N:1 mux generically?* `assign y = d[sel];` with `d` as an unpacked array of `W`-bit words.
- *One-hot select?* AND-OR: `y = |({W{s[i]}} & d[i])` across i. Assert `$onehot(s)`.

tongxin-193:
**Key idea:** Work in time-precision ticks. Compute `max = floor(1.25·T / tick)`, randomize an integer in `[0, max]`, and delay by `ticks × tick`. Integer ticks avoid real-number rounding surprises, and logging the seed makes the run reproducible. T can be a parameter, or you can measure it from the actual clock.

```wavedrom
{ "signal": [
  { "name": "clk (T=10ns)", "wave": "p....." },
  { "name": "trigger",      "wave": "010..." },
  { "name": "legal window", "wave": "0.=.0.", "data": ["0 .. 12.5ns"] },
  { "name": "delayed_sig",  "wave": "0..1.0" }
],
  "head": { "text": "delayed_sig rises at trigger + rand(0..1.25T)" } }
```

### Code
```systemverilog
`timescale 1ns/1ps
module tb;
  logic clk = 0, trig = 0, dly_sig = 0;
  realtime T;
  always #5 clk = ~clk;                     // T = 10 ns

  // Measure the period from two rising edges (robust if clk changes).
  task automatic measure_period(output realtime per);
    realtime t0;
    @(posedge clk) t0 = $realtime;
    @(posedge clk) per = $realtime - t0;
  endtask

  // Random delay in [0, 1.25*T], resolution = 1 ps
  task automatic rand_delay(realtime per);
    int unsigned max_ticks = $rtoi(1.25 * per / 1ps);   // 12500 for 10 ns
    int unsigned ticks     = $urandom_range(max_ticks, 0); // inclusive
    `ifdef DEBUG $display("%0t delay=%0d ps", $realtime, ticks); `endif
    #(ticks * 1ps);
  endtask

  initial begin
    measure_period(T);
    repeat (10) begin
      @(posedge clk) trig <= 1;
      @(posedge clk) trig <= 0;
      fork
        begin
          automatic realtime t_start = $realtime;
          rand_delay(T);
          dly_sig = 1;
          assert ($realtime - t_start <= 1.25*T)
            else $error("delay too long");
          #1 dly_sig = 0;
        end
      join
    end
    $finish;
  end
endmodule
```

### Explanation
- `$urandom_range(max, min)` is inclusive at both ends and follows the thread's random stability. Run with `+ntb_random_seed`/`-sv_seed` and print the seed.
- `1.25 * per / 1ps` converts the realtime period into an integer number of ps, independent of the module timeunit.
- A class version works too: `rand int unsigned ticks; constraint c {ticks <= max_ticks;}`. It allows `dist` weighting, for example extra weight on the endpoints 0, T and 1.25T.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Timescale: `#12.3456` in a 1ns/100ps module rounds to 100 ps. Delaying in ps ticks avoids hidden rounding.
- Cover the endpoints explicitly (0 and max) because uniform random rarely hits them.
- If the clock frequency changes at run time, re-measure T per transaction.

### Follow-ups
- *Delay in whole cycles plus a fraction?* `repeat (n) @(posedge clk); #(frac*1ps);`
- *Why not `$random % N`?* It can return negative values and is not thread-stable.

tongxin-195:
**Key idea:** Make `Shape` a `virtual class` (abstract) with `pure virtual function real area()`. Give it a concrete `print_area()` that calls `area()`. `Circle` and `Rectangle` each implement `area()`. Store them in `Shape` handles, and one loop prints the correct area for each object through dynamic dispatch.

```mermaid
classDiagram
  class Shape {
    +string name
    +area() real pure virtual
    +print_area() virtual
  }
  class Circle {
    +real r
    +area() real
  }
  class Rectangle {
    +real w
    +real h
    +area() real
  }
  Shape <|-- Circle
  Shape <|-- Rectangle
```

### Code
```systemverilog
virtual class Shape;
  string name;
  function new(string name); this.name = name; endfunction
  pure virtual function real area();
  virtual function void print_area();
    $display("%-10s area = %0.3f", name, area());   // dispatches to subclass
  endfunction
endclass

class Circle extends Shape;
  real r;
  localparam real PI = 3.14159265358979;
  function new(real r); super.new("Circle"); this.r = r; endfunction
  virtual function real area(); return PI * r * r; endfunction
endclass

class Rectangle extends Shape;
  real w, h;
  function new(real w, real h); super.new("Rectangle"); this.w = w; this.h = h; endfunction
  virtual function real area(); return w * h; endfunction
endclass

module tb;
  initial begin
    Shape shapes[$];
    Circle    c = new(2.0);
    Rectangle r = new(3.0, 4.5);
    shapes.push_back(c);
    shapes.push_back(r);
    foreach (shapes[i]) shapes[i].print_area();
    // Shape s = new("x");   // compile error: cannot instantiate a virtual class
  end
endmodule
```

### Output
| Object | Calculation | Printed |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Circle r=2 | π·2·2 | `Circle     area = 12.566` |
| Rectangle 3×4.5 | 3·4.5 | `Rectangle  area = 13.500` |

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- A non-abstract subclass that forgets to implement `area()` is a compile error. That is the point of `pure virtual`.
- `$display("%f")` formatting of reals: use `%0.3f` for a fixed number of decimals.
- The constructors call `super.new(name)` first. When the base `new` has arguments, the derived class must call it explicitly.

### Follow-ups
- *Add a Triangle?* Write a new subclass. The loop and `print_area()` stay unchanged (open/closed principle).
- *UVM link:* the same pattern appears as `uvm_sequence_item` subclasses handled through base handles.

tongxin-196:
**Key idea:** `join_none` children run only after the parent blocks, so the parent's next statement still sees the old shared state. Children that do a read, then `#delay`, then write back will overwrite each other's updates (lost updates). A semaphore makes each read-modify-write atomic. It does **not** decide which waiting child gets the key first.

### Code
```systemverilog
module tb;
  int count;
  semaphore sem = new(1);
  bit use_sem;

  task automatic incr(string name, int d);
    int tmp;
    if (use_sem) sem.get(1);
    tmp = count;              // read
    #d;                       // other threads run here
    count = tmp + 1;          // write back
    $display("%0t %s count=%0d", $time, name, count);
    if (use_sem) sem.put(1);
  endtask

  initial begin
    for (int mode = 0; mode < 2; mode++) begin
      use_sem = mode; count = 0;
      fork
        incr("A", 5);
        incr("B", 2);
        incr("C", 1);
      join_none
      $display("%0t parent sees count=%0d", $time, count);
      wait fork;
      $display("%0t mode=%0d final=%0d", $time, mode, count);
    end
  end
endmodule
```

### Trace (A, B, C start in fork order, which is typical but not guaranteed)
| mode | time | event | count |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| no sem | 0 | parent prints first | 0 |
| no sem | 0 | A, B, C all read tmp=0 | 0 |
| no sem | 1 | C writes 0+1 | 1 |
| no sem | 2 | B writes 0+1 | 1 |
| no sem | 5 | A writes 0+1 | **1 (two updates lost)** |
| sem | 5 | parent prints 0; A got key at 5, writes at 10 | 1 |
| sem | 12 | B (waited for key) writes | 2 |
| sem | 13 | C writes | **3** |

```wavedrom
{ "signal": [
  { "name": "A holds key", "wave": "01....0.....", "node": "" },
  { "name": "B holds key", "wave": "0.....1.0..." },
  { "name": "C holds key", "wave": "0.......10.." },
  { "name": "count",       "wave": "=.....=.==..", "data": ["0", "1", "2", "3"] }
],
  "head": { "text": "with semaphore: updates serialized, final = 3" } }
```

### Explanation
- The parent's `$display` runs before any child, because the parent has not yet blocked.
- The semaphore's waiting queue is FIFO, but the order in which the children reach `get()` in the same timestep is up to the scheduler. With semaphore the final value is always 3. Which name prints first is not guaranteed.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Forgetting `put()` on an early-return path deadlocks every later caller.
- `get(n)` with n greater than the initial key count blocks forever.

### Follow-ups
- *Fixed order A→B→C?* Pass a token through mailboxes or events, or use per-thread semaphores initialized to 0 and chain them.

tongxin-199:
**Key idea:** **Override** means a derived class redefines an inherited (virtual) method with the same signature, and the runtime object type picks the version. **Overload** means several functions share one name but take different parameter lists, and the compiler picks one from the argument types. C++ supports both. SystemVerilog supports overriding but **not** method overloading, so you use different names, default arguments, or parameterized classes instead.

| | Override | Overload |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Where | base vs derived class | same scope |
| Signature | identical | must differ (types/count) |
| Resolved | run time (virtual dispatch) | compile time |
| C++ | `virtual` + `override` keyword | yes |
| SystemVerilog | `virtual function` in base | **no**. One name means one prototype per class |

### Code
```cpp
// C++
#include <iostream>
struct Base {
  virtual void show() const { std::cout << "Base\n"; }
  void add(int a)           { std::cout << "int " << a << "\n"; }     // overload 1
  void add(double a)        { std::cout << "double " << a << "\n"; }  // overload 2
  virtual ~Base() = default;
};
struct Derived : Base {
  void show() const override { std::cout << "Derived\n"; }            // override
};
int main() {
  Base* p = new Derived;
  p->show();      // Derived  (runtime)
  p->add(3);      // int 3    (compile time)
  p->add(2.5);    // double 2.5
  delete p;
}
```

```systemverilog
// SystemVerilog: override works, overload does not
class base;
  virtual function void show(); $display("base"); endfunction
  // function void add(int a); ...  and  function void add(real a); ...  -> ERROR
  function void add(int a, int b = 0);           // default argument instead
    $display("%0d", a + b);
  endfunction
endclass
class derived extends base;
  virtual function void show(); $display("derived"); endfunction  // override
endclass
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- In SV the overriding method's prototype (argument types, directions, return type) must match the base exactly.
- In C++ a derived function with the same name but a different signature **hides** all base overloads, so add `using Base::add;`.
- C++ default arguments bind statically, so a virtual function with defaults uses the default from the handle's static type.

### Follow-ups
- *Operator overloading in SV?* Not supported in classes. Write a `compare()` or `do_compare()` method.
- *Polymorphism kinds?* Compile-time (overloading, templates/parameterized classes) and run-time (virtual).

tongxin-200:
**Key idea:** A variable single-bit select is just `x[i]`. A field with a **variable start** and **constant width** uses an indexed part-select: `x[base +: W]` (counting up from `base`) or `x[base -: W]` (counting down). Plain `x[msb:lsb]` needs constant bounds. If the **width** also varies at run time, use shift-and-mask, or a loop with explicit bounds.

### Worked example (x = 32'hDEAD_BEEF)
| Expression | Bits taken | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `x[4]` | bit 4 | 1'b0 (0xF = 1111 → bit4 is from 0xE: 1110 → 0) |
| `x[8 +: 8]` | x[15:8] | 8'hBE |
| `x[15 -: 8]` | x[15:8] | 8'hBE |
| `x[i*8 +: 8]`, i=3 | x[31:24] | 8'hDE |
| `(x >> 4) & ((1<<12)-1)` | x[15:4], width 12 | 12'hEEE |

### Code
```systemverilog
module bitfield_demo;
  logic [31:0] x = 32'hDEAD_BEEF;

  // fixed width, variable position (synthesizable: becomes a mux/shifter)
  function automatic logic [7:0] get_byte(logic [31:0] v, int unsigned idx);
    return v[idx*8 +: 8];
  endfunction

  // variable width AND position (width 0..32)
  function automatic logic [31:0] get_field(logic [31:0] v,
                                            int unsigned lsb, int unsigned width);
    logic [32:0] mask;                     // 33 bits so width=32 does not overflow
    mask = (33'd1 << width) - 1;
    return (v >> lsb) & mask[31:0];
  endfunction

  // variable-width write (read-modify-write)
  function automatic logic [31:0] set_field(logic [31:0] v, int unsigned lsb,
                                            int unsigned width, logic [31:0] val);
    logic [32:0] mask = ((33'd1 << width) - 1) << lsb;
    return (v & ~mask[31:0]) | ((val << lsb) & mask[31:0]);
  endfunction

  initial begin
    $display("%h", get_byte(x, 2));            // ad
    $display("%h", get_field(x, 4, 12));       // eee
    $display("%h", set_field(x, 8, 8, 8'h55)); // dead55ef
    // x[lsb+width-1:lsb]  -> compile error: bounds must be constant
  end
endmodule
```

### Explanation
- `+:` and `-:` need a constant width but allow any runtime base, which makes them perfect for byte lanes, `strb` handling and packed arrays.
- The shift-and-mask method works for any width, but beware of shifting by ≥ the word width. Use a wider mask (33 bits here) or special-case `width == 32`.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- An out-of-range index or part-select reads X (for 4-state) and the write is ignored. Assert bounds.
- With `logic [0:31] y` (ascending range), `+:` still counts from `base` toward higher indices, but those are now lower-significance bits.
- `x[i]` with `i` containing X returns X.

### Follow-ups
- *Reverse bits of a variable-width field?* Loop `for (int k=0; k<width; k++) r[k] = v[lsb+width-1-k];`, or use the streaming operator `{<<{v}}` for a fixed width.

tongxin-202:
**Key idea:** Three equivalent detectors with overlap support. A **shift register** compares the last 5 bits with `10110`. A **Mealy FSM** has 5 prefix states (none, 1, 10, 101, 1011) and raises the output combinationally on the final `0`. A **Moore FSM** adds a 6th "seen 10110" state, so its output comes one cycle later and is glitch-free. On overlap, each FSM falls back to the longest suffix that is also a prefix.

### Mealy state diagram (output on edge)
```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
  S0 --> S1: 1/0
  S0 --> S0: 0/0
  S1 --> S2: 0/0
  S1 --> S1: 1/0
  S2 --> S3: 1/0
  S2 --> S0: 0/0
  S3 --> S4: 1/0
  S3 --> S2: 0/0
  S4 --> S2: 0/1
  S4 --> S1: 1/0
```
Moore version: replace `S4 --0--> S2` with `S4 --0--> S5` (out=1), where `S5 --1--> S3` and `S5 --0--> S0`.

### Trace: input 1 0 1 1 0 1 1 0 (overlapping)
```wavedrom
{ "signal": [
  { "name": "clk",   "wave": "p........" },
  { "name": "din",   "wave": "101.01.0." },
  { "name": "state (Mealy)", "wave": "=========", "data": ["S0", "S1", "S2", "S3", "S4", "S2", "S3", "S4", "S2"] },
  { "name": "det_mealy", "wave": "0...10.10" },
  { "name": "det_moore", "wave": "0....10.1" }
],
  "head": { "text": "two overlapping matches; Moore output lags by one cycle" } }
```

### Code
```systemverilog
module det_10110 (
  input  logic clk, rst_n, din,
  output logic det_mealy, det_moore, det_shreg
);
  // ---------- Mealy FSM ----------
  typedef enum logic [2:0] {S0, S1, S2, S3, S4, S5} st_e;
  st_e st, nst;

  always_ff @(posedge clk or negedge rst_n)
    if (!rst_n) st <= S0; else st <= nst;

  always_comb begin
    nst = st;
    unique case (st)
      S0: nst = din ? S1 : S0;
      S1: nst = din ? S1 : S2;
      S2: nst = din ? S3 : S0;
      S3: nst = din ? S4 : S2;
      S4: nst = din ? S1 : S2;          // "10110" -> suffix "10"
      default: nst = S0;
    endcase
  end
  assign det_mealy = (st == S4) && !din;

  // ---------- Moore FSM ----------
  st_e mst, mnst;
  always_ff @(posedge clk or negedge rst_n)
    if (!rst_n) mst <= S0; else mst <= mnst;

  always_comb begin
    mnst = mst;
    unique case (mst)
      S0: mnst = din ? S1 : S0;
      S1: mnst = din ? S1 : S2;
      S2: mnst = din ? S3 : S0;
      S3: mnst = din ? S4 : S2;
      S4: mnst = din ? S1 : S5;
      S5: mnst = din ? S3 : S0;         // "101101" -> "101"; "101100" -> none
      default: mnst = S0;
    endcase
  end
  assign det_moore = (mst == S5);

  // ---------- Shift register ----------
  logic [3:0] sh;                        // last 4 bits
  always_ff @(posedge clk or negedge rst_n)
    if (!rst_n) sh <= '0; else sh <= {sh[2:0], din};
  assign det_shreg = ({sh, din} == 5'b10110);   // Mealy timing
endmodule
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- After reset the shift register holds zeros. The pattern starts with `1`, so zero padding cannot fake a match. A pattern like `00110` would need a "4 bits seen" counter.
- Non-overlapping variant: on a match, go to S0 (and clear the shift register) instead of S2.
- Mealy output is combinational from `din`, so it can glitch. Register it if it feeds other logic.

### Follow-ups
- *How many flops?* Mealy: 3 (5 states). Moore: 3 (6 states). Shift register: 4. For long patterns the shift register grows linearly, while the FSM needs about log2(N+1) bits.

tongxin-204:
**Key idea:** The input monitor publishes every **accepted** `(a, b)` pair, and the reference model computes the expected sum, full width including carry. The scoreboard queues expectations, and the output monitor's results are compared against them. For a fixed-latency in-order pipeline a FIFO is enough, and you can also tag each entry with its due cycle to check latency. For variable latency or out-of-order completion, match by ID. Flush on reset, and report missing, extra, and leftover items.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  IM["input monitor (valid and ready)"] -->|"a, b, id, cycle"| RM["ref model: sum = a + b"]
  RM --> Q["expected FIFO or map by id"]
  OM["output monitor (out_valid)"] -->|"sum, id, cycle"| CMP["compare"]
  Q --> CMP
  CMP --> R["errors / stats / leftovers"]
```

```wavedrom
{ "signal": [
  { "name": "clk",       "wave": "p........" },
  { "name": "in_valid",  "wave": "01.01...0" },
  { "name": "a+b",       "wave": "x==x==..x", "data": ["3+4", "5+6", "1+1", "9+9"] },
  { "name": "out_valid", "wave": "0...1.01." },
  { "name": "sum",       "wave": "x...==x==", "data": ["7", "11", "2", "18"] }
],
  "head": { "text": "LATENCY = 3: each accepted input appears 3 cycles later" } }
```

### Code
```systemverilog
class add_in_tr extends uvm_sequence_item;
  `uvm_object_utils(add_in_tr)
  bit [15:0] a, b; longint cyc;
  function new(string name = "add_in_tr"); super.new(name); endfunction
endclass
class add_out_tr extends uvm_sequence_item;
  `uvm_object_utils(add_out_tr)
  bit [16:0] sum; longint cyc;
  function new(string name = "add_out_tr"); super.new(name); endfunction
endclass

`uvm_analysis_imp_decl(_in)
`uvm_analysis_imp_decl(_out)

class add_scoreboard extends uvm_scoreboard;
  `uvm_component_utils(add_scoreboard)
  uvm_analysis_imp_in  #(add_in_tr,  add_scoreboard) in_imp;
  uvm_analysis_imp_out #(add_out_tr, add_scoreboard) out_imp;

  int unsigned LATENCY = 3;
  typedef struct { bit [16:0] sum; longint due; } exp_t;
  exp_t exp_q[$];
  int n_ok, n_err;

  function new(string name, uvm_component parent);
    super.new(name, parent);
    in_imp  = new("in_imp", this);
    out_imp = new("out_imp", this);
  endfunction

  // reference model: full-width sum, no truncation
  function void write_in(add_in_tr t);
    exp_t e;
    e.sum = {1'b0, t.a} + {1'b0, t.b};
    e.due = t.cyc + LATENCY;
    exp_q.push_back(e);
  endfunction

  function void write_out(add_out_tr t);
    exp_t e;
    if (exp_q.size() == 0) begin
      `uvm_error("UNEXP", $sformatf("unexpected sum %0d @cyc %0d", t.sum, t.cyc))
      n_err++; return;
    end
    e = exp_q.pop_front();
    if (t.sum !== e.sum) begin
      `uvm_error("DATA", $sformatf("exp %0d got %0d", e.sum, t.sum)); n_err++;
    end else if (t.cyc != e.due) begin
      `uvm_error("LAT", $sformatf("due cyc %0d, came %0d", e.due, t.cyc)); n_err++;
    end else n_ok++;
  endfunction

  function void flush();                      // call from reset monitor
    exp_q.delete();
  endfunction

  function void check_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    if (exp_q.size())
      `uvm_error("MISSING", $sformatf("%0d results never came out", exp_q.size()))
  endfunction

  function void report_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    `uvm_info("SB", $sformatf("ok=%0d err=%0d", n_ok, n_err), UVM_LOW)
  endfunction
endclass
```

### Explanation
- Feed the model from what the monitor observed (handshake accepted), not from the sequence. Then bubbles and back-pressure are handled automatically.
- If the output can stall, the fixed `due` check becomes "no earlier than LATENCY", or count only cycles where the pipeline advances.
- For out-of-order completion, store entries in `exp_t exp_m[int]` keyed by tag/ID and delete them on match. A second arrival with the same ID is a duplicate.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Overflow: compute the sum in N+1 bits, then compare either the carry-out or the truncated sum, whichever the spec defines.
- Mid-test reset: flush expectations for in-flight data that the DUT legally discards.
- All-zero operands hide stuck-at bugs. Constrain or cover max values and carry propagation patterns such as `0xFFFF + 1`.

### Follow-ups
- *Detect a dropped result early?* Do a per-cycle check: if `exp_q[0].due < current_cycle`, report it as missing now instead of waiting for end of test.

tongxin-206:
**Key idea:** Put constraints that define a **legal single transaction** in the sequence item: protocol rules, alignment, length limits. Put **scenario** choices in the sequence: how many items, address ranges for this test, relations between consecutive items, traffic mix. They then reach the item through inline `with {}` constraints. Remember that `seq.start()` does **not** randomize the sequence's own `rand` fields. Call `seq.randomize()` first, or use `` `uvm_do_with `` / `` `uvm_rand_send_with ``.

| | In `uvm_sequence_item` | In `uvm_sequence` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Scope | one transaction | stream of transactions |
| Typical | `addr` aligned to `size`, `len` 1..16, legal opcodes | `num_trans`, base address window, read-after-write pattern |
| Visible to | every sequence that uses the item | this scenario only |
| Override | `soft` / derived item + factory | inline `with`, sequence subclass |
| Randomized when | `req.randomize()` in body | `seq.randomize()` before `start` |

### Code
```systemverilog
class bus_item extends uvm_sequence_item;
  `uvm_object_utils(bus_item)
  rand bit [31:0] addr;
  rand bit [2:0]  size;           // bytes = 1<<size
  rand bit [3:0]  len;
  rand bit        write;
  constraint c_legal { size <= 2; addr % (1 << size) == 0; }   // hard: protocol
  constraint c_len_d { soft len inside {[1:4]}; }               // default, overridable
  function new(string name = "bus_item"); super.new(name); endfunction
endclass

class raw_seq extends uvm_sequence #(bus_item);
  `uvm_object_utils(raw_seq)
  rand int unsigned n;
  rand bit [31:0]   base;
  constraint c_n    { n inside {[5:20]}; }
  constraint c_base { base[11:0] == 0; }

  function new(string name = "raw_seq"); super.new(name); endfunction

  task body();
    bit [31:0] last_addr;
    repeat (n) begin
      req = bus_item::type_id::create("req");
      start_item(req);
      if (!req.randomize() with {
            addr inside {[local::base : local::base + 'hFFF]};
            write == 1 -> len == 8;           // overrides soft len
          })
        `uvm_error("RAND", "item randomize failed")
      finish_item(req);
      // read-after-write of the same address
      if (req.write) begin
        last_addr = req.addr;
        req = bus_item::type_id::create("rd");
        start_item(req);
        void'(req.randomize() with { write == 0; addr == last_addr; });
        finish_item(req);
      end
    end
  endtask
endclass

// in the test
//   raw_seq s = raw_seq::type_id::create("s");
//   if (!s.randomize() with { n == 10; }) `uvm_fatal("RAND", "seq")
//   s.start(env.agt.sqr);
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Inside `with {}`, a name such as `base` resolves to the **item** first. Use `local::` to refer to the sequence's variable.
- An inline constraint that conflicts with a hard item constraint makes `randomize()` fail. Always check the return value.
- Without `s.randomize()`, `n` keeps its default 0 and the body sends nothing.

### Follow-ups
- *Turn off an item constraint for an error test?* `req.c_legal.constraint_mode(0)` before randomize, or use a derived error item.

tongxin-207:
**Key idea:** Parameterize the sequencer and driver on a **common base item**, and derive the concrete kinds (read, write, config...) from it. Sequences send any subtype. The driver either calls virtual methods on the base handle (cleanest) or uses checked `$cast` to tell the kinds apart. Unknown types should give a clear error. If the protocols are really different, use separate agents.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  S1["write_seq"] -->|"wr_item"| SQR["uvm_sequencer #(base_item)"]
  S2["read_seq"] -->|"rd_item"| SQR
  S3["cfg_seq"] -->|"cfg_item"| SQR
  SQR -->|"base_item handle"| DRV["driver #(base_item)"]
  DRV -->|"$cast or virtual drive()"| PINS["interface"]
```

### Code
```systemverilog
virtual class base_item extends uvm_sequence_item;
  rand bit [7:0] id;
  function new(string name = "base_item"); super.new(name); endfunction
  pure virtual function string kind();
endclass

class wr_item extends base_item;
  `uvm_object_utils(wr_item)
  rand bit [31:0] addr, data;
  function new(string name = "wr_item"); super.new(name); endfunction
  virtual function string kind(); return "WR"; endfunction
endclass

class rd_item extends base_item;
  `uvm_object_utils(rd_item)
  rand bit [31:0] addr; bit [31:0] rdata;
  function new(string name = "rd_item"); super.new(name); endfunction
  virtual function string kind(); return "RD"; endfunction
endclass

typedef uvm_sequencer #(base_item) my_sqr;

class my_driver extends uvm_driver #(base_item);
  `uvm_component_utils(my_driver)
  virtual bus_if vif;
  function new(string name, uvm_component parent); super.new(name, parent); endfunction

  task run_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    wr_item w; rd_item r;
    forever begin
      seq_item_port.get_next_item(req);
      if ($cast(w, req))      drive_write(w);
      else if ($cast(r, req)) drive_read(r);      // fills r.rdata
      else `uvm_error("TYPE", {"unsupported item kind ", req.get_type_name()})
      seq_item_port.item_done();
    end
  endtask

  task drive_write(wr_item w); /* pin wiggling */ endtask
  task drive_read (rd_item r); /* pin wiggling */ endtask
endclass

class mixed_seq extends uvm_sequence #(base_item);
  `uvm_object_utils(mixed_seq)
  function new(string name = "mixed_seq"); super.new(name); endfunction
  task body();
    wr_item w = wr_item::type_id::create("w");
    rd_item r = rd_item::type_id::create("r");
    start_item(w); void'(w.randomize()); finish_item(w);
    start_item(r); void'(r.randomize() with { addr == w.addr; }); finish_item(r);
    `uvm_info("SEQ", $sformatf("read back %h", r.rdata), UVM_MEDIUM)
  endtask
endclass
```

### Explanation
- Since `req` refers to the same object the sequence created, the driver's write to `r.rdata` is visible to the sequence after `finish_item`.
- A more object-oriented alternative: give `base_item` a virtual `drive(vif)` and avoid the `$cast` chain. The trade-off is that pin knowledge then lives in the items.
- Order the `$cast` chain from the most-derived to the least-derived class if the types inherit from each other.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- `uvm_sequence #(wr_item)` cannot run on a `uvm_sequencer #(base_item)` without a mismatch. Make the sequences `#(base_item)` too.
- Factory overrides of `wr_item` still work because the driver uses `$cast` to the base of the override.

### Follow-ups
- *Responses of different types?* Use `uvm_driver #(base_item, base_item)` and `$cast` in the sequence's `get_response`.

tongxin-214:
**Key idea:** Model what the DMA **should** do from what it was **programmed** to do. Monitor descriptor/CSR writes, keep a byte-addressed reference memory, and replay each transfer (src → dst, length, increment/fixed, size) into an expected destination image. Then check the DUT's actual bus writes byte by byte (respecting strobes) and the completion status and interrupt. Track outstanding transfers by channel/ID and handle errors, abort and reset.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  CFG["APB/CSR monitor"] -->|descriptor| DM["descriptor table (by channel)"]
  RD["AXI read monitor"] -->|"src beats"| RM["ref memory (byte assoc array)"]
  DM --> PRED["predictor: expected dst bytes + status"]
  RM --> PRED
  WR["AXI write monitor"] -->|"dst beats + strb"| CHK["checker"]
  PRED --> CHK
  IRQ["IRQ / status monitor"] --> CHK
  CHK --> REP["errors, leftovers, coverage"]
```

### Code
```systemverilog
class dma_scoreboard extends uvm_scoreboard;
  `uvm_component_utils(dma_scoreboard)
  uvm_tlm_analysis_fifo #(dma_desc_tr) desc_f;   // from CSR monitor
  uvm_tlm_analysis_fifo #(axi_tr)      wr_f;     // from AXI write monitor
  uvm_tlm_analysis_fifo #(irq_tr)      irq_f;

  bit [7:0] src_mem [bit [63:0]];   // reference source memory (preloaded/backdoor)
  bit [7:0] exp_mem [bit [63:0]];   // expected destination bytes still pending
  dma_desc_tr active[int];          // channel -> descriptor in flight

  function new(string name, uvm_component parent);
    super.new(name, parent);
    desc_f = new("desc_f", this); wr_f = new("wr_f", this); irq_f = new("irq_f", this);
  endfunction

  // predict all destination bytes when a channel is started
  task predict(dma_desc_tr d);
    for (longint i = 0; i < d.length; i++) begin
      bit [63:0] s = d.src_inc ? d.src + i : d.src + (i % (1 << d.size));
      bit [63:0] t = d.dst_inc ? d.dst + i : d.dst + (i % (1 << d.size));
      if (!src_mem.exists(s)) `uvm_warning("SRC", $sformatf("uninit src %h", s))
      exp_mem[t] = src_mem.exists(s) ? src_mem[s] : 8'h00;
    end
    active[d.ch] = d;
  endtask

  task check_write(axi_tr w);
    foreach (w.data[beat]) begin
      bit [63:0] base = w.beat_addr(beat);         // handles INCR/FIXED/WRAP
      for (int b = 0; b < w.bytes_per_beat; b++) begin
        bit [63:0] a = base + b;
        if (!w.strb[beat][b]) continue;             // disabled lane
        if (!exp_mem.exists(a))
          `uvm_error("UNEXP", $sformatf("write to unexpected addr %h", a))
        else if (exp_mem[a] !== w.data[beat][8*b +: 8])
          `uvm_error("DATA", $sformatf("addr %h exp %h got %h",
                     a, exp_mem[a], w.data[beat][8*b +: 8]))
        else exp_mem.delete(a);                     // consumed
      end
    end
  endtask

  task run_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    fork
      forever begin dma_desc_tr d; desc_f.get(d); predict(d); end
      forever begin axi_tr w;      wr_f.get(w);   check_write(w); end
      forever begin irq_tr i;      irq_f.get(i);
        if (!active.exists(i.ch)) `uvm_error("IRQ", "irq for idle channel")
        else if (i.err != active[i.ch].expect_err) `uvm_error("STS", "status mismatch")
        active.delete(i.ch);
      end
    join
  endtask

  function void check_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    if (exp_mem.size()) `uvm_error("MISSING", $sformatf("%0d bytes never written", exp_mem.size()))
    if (active.size())  `uvm_error("HANG",    $sformatf("%0d channels never completed", active.size()))
  endfunction
endclass
```

### Explanation
- Byte granularity makes unaligned start/end addresses, narrow transfers and strobes trivial to check.
- The interrupt should arrive only after every expected byte of that channel has been written. Check this ordering by tagging `exp_mem` entries with a channel.
- Overlapping src/dst regions: predict using the source data as it was at descriptor start, or as the spec defines memmove semantics.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Abort or reset: delete that channel's pending bytes and expect an aborted status.
- Bus errors (SLVERR/DECERR) during a read: expect an error status, and do not expect writes for the affected bytes if the spec says so.
- 4 KB boundary crossings, zero length, maximum length.

### Follow-ups
- *Why not compare the whole memory at the end?* That misses transient wrong writes that are later overwritten, and it gives late, hard-to-debug errors. Check incrementally, and do a final sweep as well.

tongxin-215:
**Key idea:** The item describes **requested intent**: source/destination address, byte length, beat size, burst length, increment/fixed per side, channel/descriptor ID and control flags. Constraints keep it legal: aligned to the size, length a multiple of the size (if the spec requires it), within the address windows, no 4 KB crossing per burst, and no illegal src/dst overlap. Observed results (status, error, completion time) go in separate fields that are not `rand`.

### Code
```systemverilog
class dma_item extends uvm_sequence_item;
  typedef enum bit [1:0] {MEM2MEM, MEM2PER, PER2MEM} dir_e;

  // ---- requested intent ----
  rand bit [3:0]   ch;              // channel
  rand bit [15:0]  desc_id;
  rand dir_e       dir;
  rand bit [39:0]  src, dst;        // byte addresses
  rand bit [23:0]  length;          // bytes
  rand bit [2:0]   size;            // bytes/beat = 1<<size
  rand bit [7:0]   burst_len;       // beats per burst - 1 (AXI style)
  rand bit         src_inc, dst_inc;
  rand bit         irq_en, chain;

  // ---- observed / response (not randomized) ----
  bit              done, err;
  bit [1:0]        resp;
  time             t_start, t_done;

  // address windows from config
  bit [39:0] mem_lo = 40'h00_0000_0000, mem_hi = 40'h00_0FFF_FFFF;
  bit [39:0] per_fifo = 40'h00_4000_0000;
  int unsigned max_len = 1 << 20;

  constraint c_size   { size inside {[0:3]}; }                       // 1..8 bytes
  constraint c_align  { src % (1 << size) == 0; dst % (1 << size) == 0; }
  constraint c_len    { length > 0; length <= max_len;
                        length % (1 << size) == 0; }
  constraint c_burst  { burst_len inside {[0:15]}; }
  constraint c_dir    {
    dir == MEM2PER -> (dst == per_fifo && dst_inc == 0 && src_inc == 1);
    dir == PER2MEM -> (src == per_fifo && src_inc == 0 && dst_inc == 1);
    dir == MEM2MEM -> (src_inc == 1 && dst_inc == 1);
  }
  // ranges computed in 41 bits so src+length cannot wrap
  constraint c_range  {
    src_inc -> ({1'b0, src} + length - 1 <= {1'b0, mem_hi} && src >= mem_lo);
    dst_inc -> ({1'b0, dst} + length - 1 <= {1'b0, mem_hi} && dst >= mem_lo);
  }
  constraint c_no_overlap {
    dir == MEM2MEM -> ({1'b0, src} + length <= dst || {1'b0, dst} + length <= src);
  }
  constraint c_order  { solve size before length; solve dir before src, dst; }
  constraint c_dist   { length dist { [1:64] :/ 40, [65:4096] :/ 40, [4097:max_len] :/ 20 }; }

  `uvm_object_utils(dma_item)   // no field macros: implement do_copy/do_compare for speed

  function new(string name = "dma_item"); super.new(name); endfunction

  function string convert2string();
    return $sformatf("ch%0d %s src=%h(%s) dst=%h(%s) len=%0d size=%0dB burst=%0d",
      ch, dir.name(), src, src_inc ? "inc" : "fix", dst, dst_inc ? "inc" : "fix",
      length, 1 << size, burst_len + 1);
  endfunction
endclass
```

| Field group | Why |
| --- | --- |
| addresses + inc flags | DMA modes (mem↔mem, mem↔peripheral FIFO) |
| length, size, burst | protocol legality, split into bursts |
| ch, desc_id | match descriptors to completions out of order |
| status fields (non-rand) | filled from monitors/driver; never constrained |

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- `src + length` in 40 bits can wrap. The widened `{1'b0, src}` arithmetic prevents false solutions.
- The modulo constraints with a variable divisor are solver-heavy. `solve size before` helps, or express alignment as `src[2:0] & ((1<<size)-1) == 0`.
- 4 KB boundary: the driver/DMA splits bursts. Add coverage for transfers that cross it rather than forbidding them.

### Follow-ups
- *Error injection?* Use a derived `dma_err_item` with `c_align.constraint_mode(0)` and an `expect_err` flag, selected by factory override.

tongxin-217:
**Key idea:** For a large, mostly empty address space, store only the bytes that were touched: an **associative array keyed by address** in SV (`bit [7:0] mem[bit [63:0]]`) or `std::unordered_map<uint64_t, uint8_t>` in C++. Define explicitly what reading a never-written byte returns. If accesses cluster, a **page map** (a page number key holding a fixed 4 KB array) cuts per-byte overhead a lot.

| Structure | Memory for N touched bytes | Lookup | Ordered iteration | Best for |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| fixed array `mem[2**32]` | whole space | O(1) | yes | small address spaces only |
| SV assoc `mem[bit[63:0]]` | ~N entries + overhead | O(log N) or hash | yes (`first`/`next`) | general sparse model |
| C++ `unordered_map` | ~N nodes (~40+ B each) | O(1) avg | no | fast random access |
| C++ `std::map` | ~N nodes | O(log N) | yes | range dumps, nearest address |
| page map (`map<page, array<4096>>`) | pages × 4 KB | O(1) + index | by page | clustered DMA / buffers |

### Code
```systemverilog
class sparse_mem;
  typedef bit [63:0] addr_t;
  bit [7:0] mem [addr_t];
  bit [7:0] default_val = 8'h00;
  bit       warn_uninit = 1;

  function void write_byte(addr_t a, bit [7:0] d); mem[a] = d; endfunction

  function bit [7:0] read_byte(addr_t a);
    if (mem.exists(a)) return mem[a];
    if (warn_uninit) $warning("read of uninitialized addr %h", a);
    return default_val;
  endfunction

  function void write_word(addr_t a, bit [31:0] d, bit [3:0] be = 4'hF);
    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) if (be[i]) mem[a + i] = d[8*i +: 8];
  endfunction
endclass
```

```cpp
#include <array>
#include <cstdint>
#include <unordered_map>

class PagedMem {
  static constexpr uint64_t kPage = 4096;
  struct Page { std::array<uint8_t, kPage> b{}; std::array<bool, kPage> v{}; };
  std::unordered_map<uint64_t, Page> pages_;
 public:
  void write(uint64_t a, uint8_t d) {
    Page& p = pages_[a / kPage];       // creates page on first touch
    p.b[a % kPage] = d; p.v[a % kPage] = true;
  }
  bool read(uint64_t a, uint8_t& d) const {   // false = never written
    auto it = pages_.find(a / kPage);
    if (it == pages_.end() || !it->second.v[a % kPage]) return false;
    d = it->second.b[a % kPage];
    return true;
  }
};
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- The key width must cover the whole address space. An `int` key silently truncates (and is signed) for 64-bit addresses.
- Wildcard index `mem[*]` is deprecated and unordered. Use a typed key.
- In SV, reading a nonexistent assoc entry returns the default value and a warning. Using `exists()` makes the intent explicit.
- Track "written" separately from the data value if 0x00 is a legal stored value.

### Follow-ups
- *Byte vs word granularity?* Byte keys handle any strobe or alignment. Word keys are cheaper but complicate partial writes.

