```

| Location | OK? | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| tb_top initial, before `run_test()` | yes | interface instance visible; set before build |
| tb_top initial, after `run_test()` | no | never reached in time |
| inside a class | only by forwarding a vif it already got | classes cannot reference module instances |
| `uvm_config_db::set(null, "*", "vif", ...)` | works, risky | every component sees it; wrong vif may match |

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- In a test's `get`, `this` + `"env.axi_agt"` must match the scope pattern used in `set`.
- With several identical interfaces, use a `generate` loop with distinct paths or keys.
- Missing vif → `uvm_fatal` in build, not a null-handle crash at time X in `run_phase`.

### Follow-ups
- *Can a package hold the interface?* No. Pass vifs from the module via config_db (or a static class variable, which is less flexible).

tongxin-229:
**Key idea:** You cannot read back a write-only (WO) register, so verify the write through its **effect**: a downstream control signal, a behavior change, an interrupt, a status bit, or a permitted **backdoor peek** of the flop. Also check that a frontdoor read returns the specified value (0, or an error response), not the stored data. The RAL mirror only shows what the model believes, so it proves nothing about the DUT.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  SEQ["reg write seq: WO reg CTRL_CMD = v"] -->|frontdoor APB| DUT["DUT"]
  DUT -->|"side effect: start pulse / mode pins"| MON["effect monitor or SVA"]
  DUT -->|"hdl_path"| PEEK["backdoor peek (if allowed)"]
  SEQ -->|"frontdoor read"| RD["expect 0 / SLVERR per spec"]
  MON --> CHK["checker"]
  PEEK --> CHK
  RD --> CHK
```

### Code
```systemverilog
class wo_reg_test_seq extends uvm_reg_sequence #(uvm_sequence #(uvm_reg_item));
  `uvm_object_utils(wo_reg_test_seq)
  my_reg_block rm;
  virtual dut_if vif;                       // to observe side effects
  function new(string name = "wo_reg_test_seq"); super.new(name); endfunction

  task body();
    uvm_status_e st; uvm_reg_data_t v, rd, peekv;
    repeat (8) begin
      v = $urandom;                          // add directed patterns: 0, '1, walking 1s
      rm.CTRL_CMD.write(st, v, UVM_FRONTDOOR, .parent(this));
      if (st != UVM_IS_OK) `uvm_error("WO", "write failed")

      // 1) backdoor peek of the storage flop (if the hdl_path exists)
      rm.CTRL_CMD.peek(st, peekv);
      if (st == UVM_IS_OK && peekv != v)
        `uvm_error("WO", $sformatf("peek %h != written %h", peekv, v))

      // 2) observable effect: e.g. mode field drives a DUT output
      @(posedge vif.clk);
      if (vif.mode_out !== v[3:0])
        `uvm_error("WO", $sformatf("mode_out %h, expected %h", vif.mode_out, v[3:0]))

      // 3) frontdoor read must NOT leak the value
      rm.CTRL_CMD.read(st, rd, UVM_FRONTDOOR, .parent(this));
      if (rd != 0) `uvm_error("WO", $sformatf("WO reg readable: %h", rd))
    end
  endtask
endclass
```

### Explanation
- In RAL, declare the field with access `"WO"` (or `"WO1"` for write-once). The built-in `uvm_reg_bit_bash_seq` and `uvm_reg_access_seq` skip WO fields or treat them specially, so you must add a dedicated test.
- For self-clearing command registers (write 1 to start), check the one-cycle pulse with SVA: `$rose(cmd_wr && wdata[0]) |=> start_pulse ##1 !start_pulse`.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Read of a WO register: the spec may require 0, the last value or SLVERR. Check exactly that.
- With `predict` on reads, the mirror may be updated to 0 after a read, giving false mismatches later. Set `.check_on_read` off or use `UVM_NO_CHECK`.
- Byte-enable writes to a WO register: verify the unwritten bytes keep their value through the side effect.

### Follow-ups
- *No side effect and no backdoor?* Then the write is unverifiable at block level. Raise it in spec review, or verify it at the next level where the effect is visible.

tongxin-231:
**Key idea:** Without editing the class you have four options. (1) **Inline constraints** `randomize() with {...}` add constraints, and they work when they are compatible or the default is `soft`. (2) **`constraint_mode(0)`** turns off a named constraint. (3) A **derived class** redefines a constraint **with the same name**, which replaces it, and a **factory override** makes the environment use that class. (4) **`rand_mode(0)`** freezes a field, which you then assign directly.

| Method | Changes | Scope | Needs original to be... |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `with { }` inline | adds constraints | one call | `soft`, or compatible |
| `obj.c_name.constraint_mode(0)` | disables one block | that object | named constraint |
| derived class, same constraint name | replaces block | all objects via factory | created with `type_id::create` |
| `obj.f.rand_mode(0)` + assign | fixes a variable | that object | – |
| `pre_randomize` / `post_randomize` | adjust before/after | class | not really "without modifying" |

### Code
```systemverilog
class pkt extends uvm_sequence_item;
  `uvm_object_utils(pkt)
  rand bit [7:0] len;
  rand bit [1:0] kind;
  constraint c_len  { len inside {[1:64]}; }        // hard default
  constraint c_kind { soft kind == 0; }             // soft default
  function new(string name = "pkt"); super.new(name); endfunction
endclass

// (3) derived class replaces c_len by name
class big_pkt extends pkt;
  `uvm_object_utils(big_pkt)
  constraint c_len { len inside {[200:255]}; }      // same name -> overrides
  function new(string name = "big_pkt"); super.new(name); endfunction
endclass

class big_test extends base_test;
  `uvm_component_utils(big_test)
  function new(string name, uvm_component parent); super.new(name, parent); endfunction
  function void build_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    pkt::type_id::set_type_override(big_pkt::get_type());   // env unchanged
    super.build_phase(phase);
  endfunction
endclass

// (1) and (2) inside a sequence body
//   start_item(req);
//   void'(req.randomize() with { kind == 2; });            // ok: c_kind is soft
//   req.c_len.constraint_mode(0);                           // drop the hard range
//   void'(req.randomize() with { len == 100; });            // now legal
//   finish_item(req);
```

### Trace
| Attempt | Result |
| --- | --- |
| `randomize() with {len == 100;}` (c_len on) | **fails**: 100 not in 1..64 |
| `randomize() with {kind == 2;}` | ok: soft c_kind yields |
| `c_len.constraint_mode(0)` then `with {len == 100;}` | ok |
| factory override to `big_pkt` | every `pkt` now has len 200..255 |

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- A derived constraint with a **different** name is **added** to the base one, not a replacement. With conflicting ranges, randomize fails.
- `constraint_mode` is per object. A new object created later has it on again.
- Always check `randomize()`'s return value. A silent failure keeps the old values.

### Follow-ups
- *Why make defaults `soft`?* Tests can override them inline without knowing constraint names.

tongxin-233:
**Key idea:** Create a **separate sequence object per parallel thread**, each randomized with checked inline constraints. Start them inside `fork...join`, with loop variables captured in `automatic` copies, and coordinate the scenario through an event, barrier or mailbox. Raise the objection around the whole scenario, not inside each thread.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
  participant V as virtual seq
  participant W as write_seq (agent A)
  participant R as read_seq (agent B)
  V->>W: randomize with {addr in window}
  V->>R: randomize with {n == W.n}
  par fork
    V->>W: start(sqr_a)
    W->>W: writes ...
    W-->>R: trigger ev_wr_done (uvm_event)
  and
    V->>R: start(sqr_b)
    R->>R: wait_trigger(ev_wr_done)
    R->>R: reads back
  end
  V->>V: join
```

### Code
```systemverilog
class wr_seq extends uvm_sequence #(bus_item);
  `uvm_object_utils(wr_seq)
  rand bit [31:0] base; rand int unsigned n;
  uvm_event done_ev;
  constraint c { n inside {[4:16]}; base[7:0] == 0; }
  function new(string name = "wr_seq"); super.new(name); endfunction
  task body();
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) begin
      req = bus_item::type_id::create($sformatf("wr%0d", i));
      start_item(req);
      if (!req.randomize() with { write == 1; addr == local::base + 4*local::i; })
        `uvm_fatal("RAND", "wr item")
      finish_item(req);
    end
    done_ev.trigger();                       // persistent until reset
  endtask
endclass

class rd_seq extends uvm_sequence #(bus_item);
  `uvm_object_utils(rd_seq)
  bit [31:0] base; int unsigned n; uvm_event start_ev;
  function new(string name = "rd_seq"); super.new(name); endfunction
  task body();
    start_ev.wait_ptrigger();                // safe even if trigger already happened
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) begin
      req = bus_item::type_id::create($sformatf("rd%0d", i));
      start_item(req);
      if (!req.randomize() with { write == 0; addr == local::base + 4*local::i; })
        `uvm_fatal("RAND", "rd item")
      finish_item(req);
    end
  endtask
endclass

class wr_then_rd_vseq extends uvm_sequence;
  `uvm_object_utils(wr_then_rd_vseq)
  `uvm_declare_p_sequencer(my_vsqr)
  function new(string name = "wr_then_rd_vseq"); super.new(name); endfunction
  task body();
    for (int k = 0; k < 2; k++) begin       // two independent pairs
      automatic int kk = k;
      automatic wr_seq w = wr_seq::type_id::create($sformatf("w%0d", kk));
      automatic rd_seq r = rd_seq::type_id::create($sformatf("r%0d", kk));
      automatic uvm_event e = new($sformatf("ev%0d", kk));
      if (!w.randomize() with { base == 32'h1000 * (local::kk + 1); }) `uvm_fatal("RAND", "w")
      w.done_ev = e; r.start_ev = e; r.base = w.base; r.n = w.n;
      fork
        w.start(p_sequencer.sqr_a, this);
        r.start(p_sequencer.sqr_b, this);
      join_none
    end
    wait fork;
  endtask
endclass
// test: phase.raise_objection(this); vseq.start(env.vsqr); phase.drop_objection(this);
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Reusing **one** sequence object in two parallel `start()` calls is illegal and corrupts its state. Create one object per thread.
- `@ev` / `wait_trigger()` misses a trigger that already happened. `wait_ptrigger()` or `wait(ev.triggered)` handles it.
- `local::` in the inline constraint refers to the sequence variables, not the item's.
- `automatic` declarations inside the loop are needed because `join_none` children start later.

### Follow-ups
- *Use a barrier instead?* `uvm_barrier b = new("b", 2); b.wait_for();` in each thread makes both wait until both arrive.

tongxin-234:
**Key idea:** The instruction transaction carries the decoded fields (opcode, rd, rs1, rs2, immediate), the operand values and metadata (PC, cycle). Coverage is sampled on **retired** instructions from a monitor, and forwarding coverage comes from **observed pipeline signals** (forward-mux select, stall) crossed with the dependency distance. An instruction pair that merely *looks* dependent does not prove the bypass path was used.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  GEN["instr sequence (dependency-biased)"] --> DRV["imem driver / program loader"]
  CORE["pipelined core"] --> RMON["retire monitor: pc, opcode, rd, rs1, rs2, values"]
  CORE --> FMON["pipeline monitor: fwd_sel_a/b, stall"]
  RMON --> COV["instr covergroup"]
  FMON --> COV
  RMON --> ISS["ISS ref model compare"]
```

### Code
```systemverilog
typedef enum bit [3:0] {ADD, SUB, AND_, OR_, LW, SW, BEQ, JAL} op_e;
typedef enum bit [1:0] {FWD_NONE, FWD_EX_MEM, FWD_MEM_WB} fwd_e;

class instr_tr extends uvm_sequence_item;
  `uvm_object_utils(instr_tr)
  rand op_e        op;
  rand bit [4:0]   rd, rs1, rs2;
  rand bit [11:0]  imm;
  bit [31:0]       pc, rs1_val, rs2_val, rd_val;     // observed
  fwd_e            fwd_a, fwd_b;                       // observed from pipeline
  bit              stalled;
  int unsigned     dep_dist_a, dep_dist_b;             // computed by monitor
  constraint c_dep { rs1 dist { [0:3] :/ 60, [4:31] :/ 40 }; } // bias toward reuse
  function new(string name = "instr_tr"); super.new(name); endfunction
endclass

class instr_cov extends uvm_subscriber #(instr_tr);
  `uvm_component_utils(instr_cov)
  instr_tr t;

  covergroup cg;
    cp_op   : coverpoint t.op;
    cp_dist : coverpoint t.dep_dist_a { bins d1 = {1}; bins d2 = {2}; bins d3 = {3};
                                        bins far = {[4:$]}; }
    cp_fwd  : coverpoint t.fwd_a;
    cp_stall: coverpoint t.stalled;
    cp_rd0  : coverpoint (t.rd == 0) { bins x0 = {1}; }         // writes to x0
    // dependency distance must show the matching forwarding path
    x_dist_fwd : cross cp_dist, cp_fwd {
      illegal_bins bad = binsof(cp_dist.far) && !binsof(cp_fwd) intersect {FWD_NONE};
    }
    x_op_fwd : cross cp_op, cp_fwd;              // e.g. LW -> use: load-use stall
  endgroup

  function new(string name, uvm_component parent);
    super.new(name, parent); cg = new();
  endfunction

  function void write(instr_tr tr);              // called on retirement only
    t = tr; cg.sample();
  endfunction
endclass
```

| Scenario | Expected observed signals |
| --- | --- |
| `add x1,..` then `sub x2,x1,..` (dist 1) | `fwd_a = FWD_EX_MEM`, no stall |
| dist 2 | `fwd_a = FWD_MEM_WB` |
| `lw x1` then use x1 (dist 1) | 1-cycle stall, then MEM_WB forward |
| dependency on x0 | **no** forwarding (x0 is constant 0) |

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Sample at retirement. Flushed instructions (after a branch mispredict) must not count.
- The monitor computes `dep_dist` from the retired stream, by tracking the last writer of each register.
- Both operands can be forwarded from different stages at once. Cover `fwd_a × fwd_b`.

### Follow-ups
- *How do you check correctness, not just coverage?* An ISS (e.g. Spike) lock-step compare of register writes at retirement.

tongxin-235:
**Key idea:** The monitor timestamps each rising edge with `$realtime`, computes the period `t_n − t_(n−1)`, and sends `{measured, expected}` to a checker. The expected value is snapshotted for that interval, because the clock may be reprogrammed. The check is `|measured − expected| ≤ 5% × expected`. Skip the first edges after enable or frequency change (settling), add a **missing-edge watchdog**, and check duty cycle separately.

```wavedrom
{ "signal": [
  { "name": "clk",      "wave": "P..P.P...P", "period": 1 },
  { "name": "edge ts",  "wave": "=..=.=...=", "data": ["t0", "t1", "t2", "t3"] },
  { "name": "period",   "wave": "x..=.=...=", "data": ["10.0", "6.0 FAIL", "14.0 FAIL"] },
  { "name": "watchdog", "wave": "0........1" }
],
  "head": { "text": "expected 10 ns, tolerance +/-0.5 ns" } }
```

### Code
```systemverilog
class clk_period_tr extends uvm_sequence_item;
  `uvm_object_utils(clk_period_tr)
  realtime measured, expected, t_edge;
  function new(string name = "clk_period_tr"); super.new(name); endfunction
endclass

class clk_monitor extends uvm_monitor;
  `uvm_component_utils(clk_monitor)
  virtual clk_if vif;                      // vif.clk, vif.en
  uvm_analysis_port #(clk_period_tr) ap;
  realtime exp_period = 10ns;              // updated by test/config on reprogram
  int      skip_edges = 2;

  function new(string name, uvm_component parent);
    super.new(name, parent); ap = new("ap", this);
  endfunction

  task run_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    realtime last; int n = 0;
    fork
      forever begin                        // period measurement
        @(posedge vif.clk);
        if (!vif.en) begin n = 0; continue; end
        if (n++ >= skip_edges) begin
          clk_period_tr t = clk_period_tr::type_id::create("t");
          t.measured = $realtime - last; t.expected = exp_period; t.t_edge = $realtime;
          ap.write(t);
        end
        last = $realtime;
      end
      forever begin                        // missing-edge watchdog
        realtime limit;
        wait (vif.en);
        limit = 2 * exp_period;
        fork : wd
          @(posedge vif.clk);
          begin #(limit); `uvm_error("CLKMISS", $sformatf("no edge for %0t", limit)) end
        join_any
        disable wd;
      end
    join
  endtask
endclass

class clk_checker extends uvm_subscriber #(clk_period_tr);
  `uvm_component_utils(clk_checker)
  real tol = 0.05;
  int  n_ok, n_bad;
  function new(string name, uvm_component parent); super.new(name, parent); endfunction
  function void write(clk_period_tr t);
    realtime err = (t.measured > t.expected) ? t.measured - t.expected
                                             : t.expected - t.measured;
    if (err > tol * t.expected) begin
      n_bad++;
      `uvm_error("CLKPER", $sformatf("@%0t period %0.3f ns, exp %0.3f +/- %0.1f%%",
                 t.t_edge, t.measured/1ns, t.expected/1ns, tol*100))
    end else n_ok++;
  endfunction
endclass
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Timescale/precision: with 1 ns precision, a 10.4 ns period is rounded. Use 1 ps or finer.
- On frequency switching, discard the interval that spans the switch, or snapshot `exp_period` at the start edge of the interval.
- `disable wd` (a named fork) kills every activation of that block. Fine here because there is a single monitor thread, but prefer process handles in reusable code.
- Duty cycle: measure high time (posedge→negedge) separately against its own limits.

### Follow-ups
- *Average vs instantaneous?* Spread-spectrum clocks need a check on the average over a window plus a bound on each cycle.

tongxin-236:
**Key idea:** `reg.write()` builds a `uvm_reg_item` and picks the **address map** (and so the sequencer and adapter). The **adapter's `reg2bus()`** converts the item into a bus `uvm_sequence_item`, which runs on the **bus sequencer**, and the driver performs the physical write. The response is converted back via `bus2reg()` into a status. The **mirror** is updated either automatically (auto-predict) or by a **`uvm_reg_predictor`** fed from the bus monitor (explicit prediction, recommended).

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
  participant SEQ as reg sequence
  participant REG as uvm_reg (CTRL)
  participant MAP as uvm_reg_map
  participant AD as adapter
  participant SQR as bus sequencer
  participant DRV as bus driver
  participant MON as bus monitor
  participant PRED as uvm_reg_predictor
  SEQ->>REG: write(status, 32'h5, UVM_FRONTDOOR)
  REG->>MAP: do_write(uvm_reg_item)
  MAP->>AD: reg2bus(rw) -> apb_item
  MAP->>SQR: start apb_item (parent = SEQ)
  SQR->>DRV: get_next_item
  DRV->>DRV: drive PSEL/PENABLE/PWDATA
  DRV-->>SQR: item_done (+ response)
  MAP->>AD: bus2reg(apb_item) -> status
  MAP-->>SEQ: status = UVM_IS_OK
  MON->>PRED: observed apb_item (analysis port)
  PRED->>AD: bus2reg -> addr/data
  PRED->>REG: do_predict -> mirror updated
```

### Code
```systemverilog
class apb_reg_adapter extends uvm_reg_adapter;
  `uvm_object_utils(apb_reg_adapter)
  function new(string name = "apb_reg_adapter");
    super.new(name);
    supports_byte_enable = 0;
    provides_responses   = 0;
  endfunction
  virtual function uvm_sequence_item reg2bus(const ref uvm_reg_bus_op rw);
    apb_item t = apb_item::type_id::create("t");
    t.write = (rw.kind == UVM_WRITE);
    t.addr  = rw.addr;
    t.wdata = rw.data;
    return t;
  endfunction
  virtual function void bus2reg(uvm_sequence_item bus_item, ref uvm_reg_bus_op rw);
    apb_item t;
    if (!$cast(t, bus_item)) `uvm_fatal("ADAPT", "not an apb_item")
    rw.kind   = t.write ? UVM_WRITE : UVM_READ;
    rw.addr   = t.addr;
    rw.data   = t.write ? t.wdata : t.rdata;
    rw.status = t.slverr ? UVM_NOT_OK : UVM_IS_OK;
  endfunction
endclass

// env::connect_phase
//   rm.default_map.set_sequencer(apb_agt.sqr, adapter);
//   rm.default_map.set_auto_predict(0);
//   predictor.map = rm.default_map; predictor.adapter = adapter;
//   apb_agt.mon.ap.connect(predictor.bus_in);

// in a sequence
//   uvm_status_e st;
//   rm.CTRL.write(st, 32'h5, .parent(this));
//   if (st != UVM_IS_OK) `uvm_error("REG", "CTRL write failed")
```

### Explanation
- With auto-predict, the mirror is updated from what RAL *sent*, so writes by other masters are invisible. With an explicit predictor, the mirror follows what the monitor actually *observed*.
- A field-level `write` of one field becomes a full-register bus write (read-modify-write if needed) unless byte enables or individually accessible fields are supported.
- `UVM_BACKDOOR` skips the adapter and bus and uses `hdl_path` deposits, in zero time.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- No `set_sequencer` on the map → "no sequencer" fatal on the first access.
- The bus item must report `slverr` for `status` to be meaningful.
- With `provides_responses = 1`, the driver must send a response (`put_response`), or the access hangs.

### Follow-ups
- *Where is `rw.byte_en` used?* For AXI/AHB strobes when `supports_byte_enable = 1`.

tongxin-237:
**Key idea:** Generate each half-period from a seeded random draw inside spec bounds, rather than with a fixed `#5`. Pick the **jitter model** the spec asks for: **period (cycle) jitter** (each period = T ± δ, independent), **edge (time-interval-error) jitter** (each edge = ideal n·T ± δ, no accumulation) or **wander** (slow drift). Then check minimum pulse widths, the average frequency and the jitter bounds with a monitor.

```wavedrom
{ "signal": [
  { "name": "ideal clk", "wave": "p......." },
  { "name": "jittered",  "wave": "P......." , "phase": 0.15 },
  { "name": "period",    "wave": "=.=.=.=.", "data": ["10.2", "9.7", "10.1", "9.9"] }
],
  "head": { "text": "T = 10 ns +/- 0.3 ns, each edge independently perturbed" } }
```

### Code
```systemverilog
`timescale 1ns/1ps
module jitter_clk_gen #(
  parameter realtime T_NOM   = 10ns,
  parameter realtime JIT_PK  = 0.3ns,    // peak deviation
  parameter real     DUTY    = 0.5,
  parameter realtime MIN_PW  = 4ns,      // minimum pulse width
  parameter bit      EDGE_TIE = 1        // 1 = edge jitter (no accumulation), 0 = period jitter
)(output logic clk);

  int unsigned seed;
  initial begin
    if (!$value$plusargs("clk_seed=%d", seed)) seed = 1;
    $display("jitter_clk_gen seed=%0d", seed);
  end

  // uniform real in [-1, 1] using the module's own seed (reproducible)
  function automatic real urand_pm1();
    return 2.0 * ($dist_uniform(seed, 0, 1_000_000) / 1_000_000.0) - 1.0;
  endfunction

  initial begin
    realtime t_ideal = 0, t_last = 0, target, hi;
    clk = 0;
    forever begin
      // 1) choose the absolute time of the next rising edge
      if (EDGE_TIE) begin
        t_ideal += T_NOM;                             // ideal grid n*T
        target = t_ideal + JIT_PK * urand_pm1();      // bounded error, no accumulation
      end else begin
        target = t_last + T_NOM + JIT_PK * urand_pm1(); // period jitter: phase random-walks
      end
      if (target - $realtime < MIN_PW) target = $realtime + MIN_PW;  // keep low pulse legal
      #(target - $realtime);
      clk = 1; t_last = $realtime;
      // 2) high time with clamped duty
      hi = T_NOM * DUTY;
      if (hi < MIN_PW) hi = MIN_PW;
      #(hi);
      clk = 0;
    end
  end
endmodule
```

### Checker sketch
```systemverilog
localparam realtime LIM = EDGE_TIE ? 2*JIT_PK : JIT_PK;
realtime t_last, per, sum; int n;
always @(posedge clk) begin
  if (n > 0) begin
    per = $realtime - t_last; sum += per;
    // period mode: T +/- JIT_PK; edge (TIE) mode: T +/- 2*JIT_PK
    assert (per >= T_NOM - LIM && per <= T_NOM + LIM)
      else $error("period %0.3f out of range", per);
  end
  t_last = $realtime; n++;
end
final $display("average period = %0.4f ns", sum / (n - 1));
```

### Explanation
| Model | Error accumulates? | Use |
| --- | --- | --- |
| period jitter | yes (random walk of phase) | stress per-cycle timing / CDC |
| edge jitter (TIE) | no (bounded around grid) | PLL-like source, frequency must average exact |
| wander / SSC | slow, deterministic | spread-spectrum, drift |

- In TIE mode, each edge error is bounded by ±JIT_PK, so one period can deviate by up to ±2·JIT_PK, but the average frequency is exact. In period mode, each period is within ±JIT_PK, but the phase error grows like a random walk.
- Use `$dist_uniform(seed, …)` or a class with `$urandom` for reproducibility. Print the seed.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Time precision must be finer than the jitter (1 ps here). Otherwise the jitter rounds away.
- Clamp pulse widths: an extreme draw must not create a glitch that is narrower than legal.
- Jitter on a clock used by both the TB and the DUT can create TB race conditions. Drive inputs through clocking blocks.

### Follow-ups
- *Gaussian jitter?* `$dist_normal(seed, 0, sigma_ps)` with truncation at ±k·σ.

tongxin-238:
**Key idea:** **Frontdoor** accesses go through the real bus: the adapter, sequencer, driver and DUT decode logic. They take real time and exercise address decoding, protocol, access policies and side effects. **Backdoor** accesses read or write the register's storage directly through its HDL path (`uvm_hdl_deposit`/`read`, or a user backdoor) in zero time, bypassing the bus. Use frontdoor to verify the register interface. Use backdoor for fast initialization, peeking without side effects, and checking that a frontdoor write landed in the right flop.

| | Frontdoor | Backdoor |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Path | map → adapter → sequencer → driver → bus | `hdl_path` → simulator VPI/DPI |
| Time | consumes bus cycles | zero time |
| Tests decode / protocol | yes | no |
| Side effects (clear-on-read, W1C, FIFO pop) | yes, real | `read`/`write` mimic policy; `peek`/`poke` do not |
| Needs | adapter + sequencer | correct HDL paths, tool access (+acc / -access) |
| Works in GLS / emulation | yes | paths may be renamed or optimized away |
| Typical use | access tests, normal traffic | preload, quick config, peek to cross-check |

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  T["reg.write / read"] -->|UVM_FRONTDOOR| MAP["map + adapter"] --> BUS["bus agent"] --> DUT["DUT bus slave + decode"] --> FLOP["reg flops"]
  T -->|UVM_BACKDOOR| HDL["hdl_path deposit/read"] --> FLOP
```

### Code
```systemverilog
uvm_status_e st; uvm_reg_data_t v;

// frontdoor write, then backdoor peek to prove the decode hit the right flop
rm.CTRL.write(st, 32'h1234, UVM_FRONTDOOR, .parent(this));
rm.CTRL.peek (st, v);                      // backdoor, no side effects
if (v != 32'h1234) `uvm_error("DECODE", $sformatf("CTRL holds %h", v))

// backdoor poke, then frontdoor read to check the read mux
rm.STATUS.poke(st, 32'hA5A5);
rm.STATUS.read(st, v, UVM_FRONTDOOR, .parent(this));

// HDL path setup in the register block build()
//   add_hdl_path("tb_top.u_dut.u_regs");
//   CTRL.add_hdl_path_slice("ctrl_q", 0, 32);
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- `uvm_reg_mem_hdl_paths_seq` validates all paths. Run it first, because a wrong path makes every backdoor access silently useless.
- A backdoor write while the design is also updating the flop in the same cycle: deposits can be overwritten. Do it when the design is idle.
- Built-in `uvm_reg_access_seq` uses frontdoor writes and backdoor reads (and the reverse) to cross-check both paths.

### Follow-ups
- *peek/poke vs read/write with UVM_BACKDOOR?* `read`/`write` with backdoor apply the field access policy (e.g. W1C emulation) and update the mirror. `peek`/`poke` are raw.

tongxin-239:
**Key idea:** An allocation transaction has a size, alignment, region/bank, and the chosen address. The constraint solver picks an address that is aligned, inside an allowed region, and **does not overlap** any live allocation. The live allocations live in a reference allocator (a queue of `{base, size}`). All address arithmetic is widened so `base + size` cannot wrap. Define release, exhaustion (randomize fails → expected allocation-failure path) and reserved regions explicitly.

### Worked example (region 0x0000–0xFFFF, alignment 0x100)
| Live allocations | New request size=0x300 | Legal bases |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [0x0000,0x0FFF] [0x2000,0x20FF] | 0x300 | 0x1000..0x1D00 (≤0x2000−0x300), 0x2100..0xFD00 |
| reserved [0x8000,0x8FFF] | – | excludes 0x7E00..0x8F00 |

### Code
```systemverilog
class ddr_alloc_tr extends uvm_sequence_item;
  `uvm_object_utils(ddr_alloc_tr)
  typedef struct { bit [39:0] base; bit [39:0] size; } range_t;

  rand bit [39:0] base;
  rand bit [39:0] size;
  rand bit [3:0]  align_log2;          // alignment = 1 << align_log2 (bytes, x256)
  rand bit [2:0]  bank;

  range_t live[$];                     // filled by the allocator model before randomize
  range_t reserved[$];
  bit [39:0] region_lo = 40'h0, region_hi = 40'hF_FFFF_FFFF;   // inclusive
  bit [39:0] bank_size = 40'h2_0000_0000;                      // 8 banks

  constraint c_size  { size inside {[40'h100 : 40'h100_0000]}; size % 40'h100 == 0; }
  constraint c_align { align_log2 inside {[8:12]};               // 256 B .. 4 KB
                       (base & ((40'd1 << align_log2) - 1)) == 0; }
  constraint c_reg   { base >= region_lo;
                       {1'b0, base} + size - 1 <= {1'b0, region_hi}; }   // 41-bit math
  constraint c_bank  { base / bank_size == bank;                 // whole buffer in one bank
                       ({1'b0, base} + size - 1) / bank_size == bank; }
  constraint c_live  { foreach (live[i])
                         ({1'b0, base} + size <= live[i].base) ||
                         (base >= {1'b0, live[i].base} + live[i].size); }
  constraint c_rsv   { foreach (reserved[i])
                         ({1'b0, base} + size <= reserved[i].base) ||
                         (base >= {1'b0, reserved[i].base} + reserved[i].size); }
  constraint c_order { solve size before base; solve align_log2 before base; }

  function new(string name = "ddr_alloc_tr"); super.new(name); endfunction
endclass

class ddr_allocator;                    // reference model
  ddr_alloc_tr::range_t live[$];
  function bit alloc(ddr_alloc_tr t);
    t.live = live;
    if (!t.randomize()) return 0;       // exhaustion / fragmentation
    live.push_back('{t.base, t.size});
    return 1;
  endfunction
  function void free(bit [39:0] base);
    int idx[$] = live.find_first_index(r) with (r.base == base);
    if (idx.size() == 0) $error("double free / unknown base %h", base);
    else live.delete(idx[0]);
  endfunction
endclass
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Overlap test with `[a, a+s)` half-open intervals: no overlap iff `a1+s1 <= a2 || a2+s2 <= a1`.
- Many live allocations make `foreach` constraints slow. Alternatively, randomize a free-gap index and an offset inside that gap.
- Cover: allocation at region start and end, exactly filling a gap, bank boundary, exhaustion, free followed by reuse of the same address.

### Follow-ups
- *Fragmentation metric?* Largest free gap ÷ total free. Bias the tests to create small gaps and then request big blocks.

tongxin-240:
**Key idea:** `uvm_component` is a permanent part of the testbench **hierarchy**. It has a parent, takes part in **phases**, and owns ports and config scope. `uvm_object` is **data**: transactions, sequences, configs. It is created and destroyed freely and has no phases or parent. Field automation macros give you `copy/compare/print/pack` for free at a runtime cost. `copy()` fills an **existing** object, and `clone()` = `create` a new object + `copy`, returned as a `uvm_object` you must `$cast`.

| | `uvm_object` | `uvm_component` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Constructor | `new(string name="")` | `new(string name, uvm_component parent)` |
| Hierarchy / full path | no | yes |
| Phases | no | build..final |
| Lifetime | dynamic | whole simulation (created in build) |
| Examples | seq_item, sequence, config | driver, monitor, env, test |
| `clone()` | yes | no (components are not cloned) |

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  A["a (existing)"] -->|"b.copy(a)"| B["b: existing object overwritten"]
  A -->|"$cast(c, a.clone())"| C["c: new object, same contents"]
```

### Code
```systemverilog
class pkt extends uvm_sequence_item;
  rand bit [7:0] addr;
  rand byte      data[];
  `uvm_object_utils(pkt)                 // no field macros: implement do_* by hand
  function new(string name = "pkt"); super.new(name); endfunction

  virtual function void do_copy(uvm_object rhs);
    pkt r;
    if (!$cast(r, rhs)) `uvm_fatal("COPY", "type mismatch")
    super.do_copy(rhs);
    addr = r.addr;
    data = r.data;                       // dynamic array assignment copies elements
  endfunction

  virtual function bit do_compare(uvm_object rhs, uvm_comparer comparer);
    pkt r;
    if (!$cast(r, rhs)) return 0;
    return super.do_compare(rhs, comparer) && addr == r.addr && data == r.data;
  endfunction

  virtual function string convert2string();
    return $sformatf("addr=%h len=%0d", addr, data.size());
  endfunction
endclass

// usage
//   pkt a = pkt::type_id::create("a"), b = pkt::type_id::create("b"), c;
//   void'(a.randomize());
//   b.copy(a);                 // b must already exist
//   $cast(c, a.clone());       // new object; c != a (different handles)
//   if (!c.compare(a)) `uvm_error("CMP", "clone differs")
```

### Explanation
- Field macros (`` `uvm_field_int(addr, UVM_ALL_ON) ``) are quick to write but expand into large generic code. Hand-written `do_copy`/`do_compare`/`do_print` are faster and allow custom rules (ignore fields, tolerance).
- `copy()` is a deep copy only for what `do_copy` copies. Nested object handles need an explicit `clone` or `copy` inside `do_copy`, or they stay shared.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Monitors must send a new object (or a clone) per transaction. Reusing one handle means every subscriber sees it change.
- `b.copy(a)` with `b == null` crashes. Use `clone()` when you don't have a target.
- Forgetting `super.do_copy(rhs)` drops base-class fields.

### Follow-ups
- *Shallow vs deep?* SV `b = new a;` is a shallow copy (nested handles shared). UVM `copy` is as deep as your `do_copy` makes it.
